HTML and JavaScript are two different programming languages used for building websites and web applications.

HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is used for structuring and formatting content on a website. It is used to create the structure and layout of a website, including headings, paragraphs, images, and links. HTML is a static language, meaning that it only displays the content that is written in the code, and it doesn't allow for dynamic changes to the content.

JavaScript, on the other hand, is a programming language that is used to create interactive and dynamic effects on a website. It is used to add functionality to a website, such as animations, pop-up windows, and form validation. JavaScript is a dynamic language, meaning that it can manipulate the content of a website and respond to user interactions.

In summary, HTML is used for structuring and formatting content, while JavaScript is used for adding interactivity and dynamic effects to a website.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

HTML:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>My Website</title>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
	<p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>
	<img src="image.jpg" alt="An image on my website">
</body>
</html>

This code creates a basic webpage with a title, a heading, a paragraph of text, and an image. The content is static and doesn't change when the page is loaded.

JavaScript:

// A JavaScript code that adds a button to the page and
// changes the text of the paragraph when the button is clicked

// Get a reference to the paragraph element
var para = document.querySelector('p');

// Get a reference to the button element
var button = document.querySelector('button');

// Add an event listener to the button
button.addEventListener('click', function() {
  // Change the text of the paragraph
  para.textContent = 'The button was clicked!';
});

This code adds a button to the page and changes the text of a paragraph when the button is clicked. The content of the page changes dynamically when the button is clicked, and this is achieved using JavaScript.

In summary, HTML is used for structuring and formatting content, while JavaScript is used for adding interactivity and dynamic effects to a website.

