One way to prove that every set is countable is to use Cantor's diagonal argument. Cantor's diagonal argument is a proof technique that can be used to show that certain sets are countable. It works by creating a new diagonal sequence that is different from the previous one, and then pairing each element in the new diagonal sequence with a unique natural number. This creates a one-to-one correspondence between the set and the natural numbers, proving that the set is countable.

